        "analysis": {
          "request": {
            "format": "tracker-fit-request",
            "version": 1,
            "requestId": "bc0cd3bf-ca8a-4ebc-b95e-9d45c8a9d275",
            "snapshotId": "89d1a244-0e8f-42be-9020-026579ed1132",
            "source": {
              "application": "Published literature example",
              "version": "Data Tool 2027 example 2026-09-21",
              "context": "Southworth et al. (2022), A search for transit timing variations in the HATS-18 planetary system, MNRAS 515, 3212; DOI 10.1093/mnras/stac1931; https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05873. All 32 times and quoted uncertainties from Table 3 are retained, in published row order, including the two TESS sector timings. Original BJD(TDB), uncertainty in days, integer cycle E, and source are preserved in the source table. Plot x = E/1000 and y = 86400 * [T - 2458626.511225 - 0.83784382*E] seconds, using the fixed reference ephemeris in section 4.3. Decimal arithmetic was used for this transformation. Subtracting this fixed line is a change of coordinates: the fitted intercept and slope remain free in every candidate. Models: linear (constant period), quadratic (constant period change per orbit), cubic (changing period evolution). All observations are included; supplied absolute 1-sigma timing uncertainties are used, with Gaussian independence and model adequacy treated as conditional assumptions. Table 4 published AIC = 61.3, 63.1, 62.9 and BIC = 64.2, 67.5, 68.7 (linear, quadratic, cubic). The paper uses chi-squared + 2k and chi-squared + k*ln(n). Data Tool retains Gaussian normalization, so absolute AIC/BIC share a common offset; delta AIC = 0, 1.80685, 1.57571. Reduced chi-squared in Data Tool uses n-k: approximately 1.90983, 1.96903, 1.95967. The paper's linear value 1.79 appears to use chi-squared/n. Parameter uncertainties here retain the supplied absolute sigma scale; the paper inflates ephemeris uncertainties. Linear is preferred modestly by AIC; these rankings do not prove a constant orbital period. SECOND SESSION: Adds two independent El Sauce CDK20 transits observed on 2024-05-08 and 2024-05-29, published by Kutluay et al. (2026), Chasing the Tides, arXiv:2602.01446v1, Tables 2 and 3: https://arxiv.org/html/2602.01446v1 . BJD(TDB) 2460439.60668 +/- 0.00082 d and 2460460.55349 +/- 0.00086 d, corresponding to original ephemeris cycles 2164 and 2189. Both added observations are present and initially unchecked in every candidate; all three fits retain exactly the original 32 selected observations and original parameter values. The newer paper also reanalyses historical and TESS observations; those are not appended here to avoid duplicating or replacing the original sample. Check the two new rows to include them in a subsequent refit. The original sample was not used to estimate or rescale the new timing uncertainties. Companion CSV: hats18-model-comparison.csv. Complete references: [S2022] Southworth, John; Barker, A. J.; Hinse, T. C.; Jongen, Y.; Dominik, M.; Jørgensen, U. G.; Longa-Peña, P.; Sajadian, S.; Snodgrass, C.; Tregloan-Reed, J.; Bach-Møller, N.; Bonavita, M.; Bozza, V.; Burgdorf, M. J.; Jaimes, R. Figuera; Helling, Ch.; Hitchcock, J. A.; Hundertmark, M.; Khalouei, E.; Korhonen, H.; Mancini, L.; Peixinho, N.; Rahvar, S.; Rabus, M.; Skottfelt, J.; Spyratos, P. (2022). A search for transit timing variations in the HATS-18 planetary system. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society, 515(3), 3212. DOI: 10.1093/mnras/stac1931. https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stac1931 . Data: Table 3; model comparison: Table 4; reference ephemeris: section 4.3. Exact manuscript used: https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05873v1 . [K2026] Kutluay, A. C.; Baştürk, Ö.; Barker, Adrian J.; Yalçınkaya, S.; Southworth, J.; Selam, S. O.; Şimşir, Ö.; Kaplan, K.; Akar, F.; Ertürk, İ. A.; Zengin, Z.; Akalın, E.; Özsoy, V.; Yaldır, Ö.; İçöz, D.; Mancini, L.; Duru, B.; Tezcan, F.; Özfidan, A.; Umar, U.; Wünsche, A.; Burgdorf, M. J.; Cannon, R. E.; Jaimes, R. J. Figuera; Hinse, T. C.; Okoth, V.; Reed, J. T.; Buğday, E. S.; Akdere, U.; Turan, Y.; Aliş, S.; Tezcan, C. T.; Yelkenci, F. K.; Hajarat, S. (2026). Chasing the Tides: Searching for Orbital Decay Signatures in Transit Timing Data and Tidal Models for 20 Hot Jupiters. arXiv:2602.01446v1, submitted 1 February 2026 (manuscript reports accepted for publication in Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific). DOI: 10.48550/arXiv.2602.01446. https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.01446 . Data: Table 3; observing dates/instrument: Table 2 and section III.1.5. Exact manuscript used: https://arxiv.org/html/2602.01446v1 .",
